[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-126850"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/assets/b37c10064003374c146c58bba99ff55304529ee66c9fb38c44246ccc90e8f402.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j$ and the square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ shown above. If the flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{1}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $x$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $n=i.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/assets/c4c4f2f3d5b6188ac5a6ec41bb96fc0c7132cc39308cebfa16ee759eabac2c75.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}({x}^{2}\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-127053/selection/5792f2c1eaf1b5cef59a211a7e9740b737c98c46c45c39b2f4a64d34a9355731" :answer/id #uuid "c6ea3d1b-b8d0-5748-841f-b094efde515c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{x}^{3}{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127053/selection/6fb92debcb7195d7ea647fd7cf0f0f0799708ebb51fc65b5f16259ae2bdc49f5" :answer/id #uuid "87df86a4-54e8-508d-aab8-0bdec254c6ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127053/selection/994ad26e04e4b36664b0e4ebbda7c38de8adecf8654a972f57a7b87f506a89fa" :answer/id #uuid "e617e31a-9325-59cb-b29a-bc8f1ae2b172" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{3}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127053/selection/646e4a54209950a7d8d4d10961965b14d0a7d823bce441331cd123987273ec36" :answer/id #uuid "08c9acbf-86ef-524a-8651-e1905ebad3e1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-x{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127053/selection/4db5e8eff5f1a28c171aae887882e0beb38cd1d2adc84dced44f9460e4816400" :answer/id #uuid "70ce25c1-89c9-5ab4-a3dd-d1500999bfbb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{x}^{3}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127053/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "97a95df4-4595-5700-aa15-ef9aa524562f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-127053/selection/4db5e8eff5f1a28c171aae887882e0beb38cd1d2adc84dced44f9460e4816400" :answer-field/choices ["q-127053/selection/5792f2c1eaf1b5cef59a211a7e9740b737c98c46c45c39b2f4a64d34a9355731" "q-127053/selection/6fb92debcb7195d7ea647fd7cf0f0f0799708ebb51fc65b5f16259ae2bdc49f5" "q-127053/selection/994ad26e04e4b36664b0e4ebbda7c38de8adecf8654a972f57a7b87f506a89fa" "q-127053/selection/646e4a54209950a7d8d4d10961965b14d0a7d823bce441331cd123987273ec36" "q-127053/selection/4db5e8eff5f1a28c171aae887882e0beb38cd1d2adc84dced44f9460e4816400"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-127053" :question/id #uuid "399b0f43-066e-5c14-a2a9-0d532f977caf" :question/math-academy-id "q-127053" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/assets/4d9663a0a40f3c3860cce0712e9e9aab41d55251ea15fca33ec663515af57d10.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{3}y\\,i-x{y}^{2}\\,j$ and the rectangle $C,$ shown above. If the flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{3}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $x$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $n=-i.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/assets/c6dd493e02633854184be1be0f38246e1e9dce2949f9cc1aa93b943c91d1e44c.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}({x}^{3}y\\,i-x{y}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (-i)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}-{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $-{x}^{3}y.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-127053/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9c8ca56-5ea8-547c-80ab-6b1f1347447e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127053"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-226126/selection/0674b229b15216f6ea870ccaaf87984357f5d6af66421aad0753c15d921d419a" :answer/id #uuid "af200a91-387e-5092-8ed6-16245b09067d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "64" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-226126/selection/d144965a68a71154d2438737e51156d9918da199ac2f07b96e885a6c60965580" :answer/id #uuid "d464ee90-50ea-5bd1-accb-64d217108f4e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "256" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-226126/selection/772faee3af740960f7d296ca245df4da797a9e413368076cbaa828915d92739d" :answer/id #uuid "3604b747-edfc-5408-aa72-5f0d2fe68c0a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "128" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-226126/selection/49f58b98d094bc5718e9bca57bba828bf6c24ca0f902b8bf7f0a9e936e7372e6" :answer/id #uuid "7ef4c183-cd0a-51fa-bc95-7019860c3c35" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "288" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-226126/selection/bdda2450817a39aa33f63c44268d508ac25aa607d31de284a7a3098288a61525" :answer/id #uuid "1585c90e-2dab-5e64-aec7-269ec7ef3c64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "72" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-226126/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b8e8ed30-9e50-5012-b659-ccdafefd0f09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-226126/selection/d144965a68a71154d2438737e51156d9918da199ac2f07b96e885a6c60965580" :answer-field/choices ["q-226126/selection/0674b229b15216f6ea870ccaaf87984357f5d6af66421aad0753c15d921d419a" "q-226126/selection/d144965a68a71154d2438737e51156d9918da199ac2f07b96e885a6c60965580" "q-226126/selection/772faee3af740960f7d296ca245df4da797a9e413368076cbaa828915d92739d" "q-226126/selection/49f58b98d094bc5718e9bca57bba828bf6c24ca0f902b8bf7f0a9e936e7372e6" "q-226126/selection/bdda2450817a39aa33f63c44268d508ac25aa607d31de284a7a3098288a61525"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-226126" :question/id #uuid "580feb93-1f7c-52cc-bad0-610745640670" :question/math-academy-id "q-226126" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/assets/6f827dac7ecaf3a659245d1178aa39a519ae3a40339c917004247f12554c871a.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=2x\\,i+2y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $8$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/assets/0b753318ebad9270c3fc8788d0e3949ffb29a800eb3f8dd6fb67cb271be68033.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(2x\\,i+2y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=4\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-4,4].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =2{\\int }_{-4}^{4}4\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8(4-(-4)) \\\\  & =8\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=4\\cdot 64=256.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-226126/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "349c29ae-5eb5-5454-a98f-c1adac3d7108"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-226126"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-226133/selection/23191a7abb2085fa58cfcf0bd067eedb8c4feb2e427bc3ee2024594244bb9b18" :answer/id #uuid "d4b764d4-8ff5-5cd1-8956-3376bbe210ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "180" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-226133/selection/d08646c2f50451273289f70234ad37461708726d9b49076c584b9c56e3de2a39" :answer/id #uuid "e9c60e56-bee5-5240-bd3d-20144d704645" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "156" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-226133/selection/e1bbe3c37664a339795d726afd32992ccbe5207280f46d0062ea5aca98f1e757" :answer/id #uuid "f975eadf-2095-52f6-9e7b-b67af944c01c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "176" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-226133/selection/9316c20d5932945ad3241a734ed10f0c90d9a4e8dde3b34950e4946d6dfd39c2" :answer/id #uuid "55878f72-f35c-51ad-8a28-3eaca9a85e46" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "164" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-226133/selection/ea469f3e53b818ab5b703dc09f193c7093ac0666865c0a5b323c43d7b199b090" :answer/id #uuid "a904007b-d324-56ec-9feb-6d521fe9c00c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "144" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-226133/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7b7971a2-f9e4-5fbf-9193-7fdfb754b51c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-226133/selection/ea469f3e53b818ab5b703dc09f193c7093ac0666865c0a5b323c43d7b199b090" :answer-field/choices ["q-226133/selection/23191a7abb2085fa58cfcf0bd067eedb8c4feb2e427bc3ee2024594244bb9b18" "q-226133/selection/d08646c2f50451273289f70234ad37461708726d9b49076c584b9c56e3de2a39" "q-226133/selection/e1bbe3c37664a339795d726afd32992ccbe5207280f46d0062ea5aca98f1e757" "q-226133/selection/9316c20d5932945ad3241a734ed10f0c90d9a4e8dde3b34950e4946d6dfd39c2" "q-226133/selection/ea469f3e53b818ab5b703dc09f193c7093ac0666865c0a5b323c43d7b199b090"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-226133" :question/id #uuid "189fc286-2334-55b5-beea-f2158d1888cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-226133" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/assets/2dcfb8778adf7e9a05ff1dff886d0d8290427ed2ae910b46db4fe9aeb36170eb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=x\\,i+3y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $6$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/assets/f283fe675e0a9ef736947e404f03ab5afb90a4209ba1a6949d18ac6d3fb20048.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(x\\,i+3y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}3\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=18.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(x\\,i+3y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+3\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =3{\\int }_{-3}^{3}3\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =54.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=54.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 18+2\\cdot 54=144.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-226133/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "446c5e28-00d3-5a17-ad90-0e66b5ec3a59"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-226133"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-226150/selection/a20b3c9808c291c56ec5bfc5f0f93a856fc692714ca7ebd36c5e4bc361f7075c" :answer/id #uuid "ab7659e8-64d7-5af0-b2e6-388983dbc232" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "42" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-226150/selection/713bb3b087b31554fba8b48520fba2f9d4d353e9b2d87f3d41c293daf48e1776" :answer/id #uuid "585b82ef-e6e9-5f60-8a82-3e64d405f965" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-226150/selection/a38ef12f135c1e6010d68ed21f81bc27372a73a756455b7066f6182c46018cc8" :answer/id #uuid "21cc8b7c-0217-50cb-aaf9-f6b4181d69fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-226150/selection/d6944c4d51ee6e8916d5e2815a8478bad91a2f9e9e9bcd499b503b9fbcb165ab" :answer/id #uuid "82414427-a2d0-51a2-9d6a-dbf2d1b16ee4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "54" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-226150/selection/cee37ae0a2921bd3973a5cb0592c822dbbfafcfefba8d7007910ead797b9112f" :answer/id #uuid "3e5f6b06-b6bf-5ff0-94d1-46b5ad229d8d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "48" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-226150/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "300c6f51-3106-536a-89c6-b3f0ee473aa4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-226150/selection/cee37ae0a2921bd3973a5cb0592c822dbbfafcfefba8d7007910ead797b9112f" :answer-field/choices ["q-226150/selection/a20b3c9808c291c56ec5bfc5f0f93a856fc692714ca7ebd36c5e4bc361f7075c" "q-226150/selection/713bb3b087b31554fba8b48520fba2f9d4d353e9b2d87f3d41c293daf48e1776" "q-226150/selection/a38ef12f135c1e6010d68ed21f81bc27372a73a756455b7066f6182c46018cc8" "q-226150/selection/d6944c4d51ee6e8916d5e2815a8478bad91a2f9e9e9bcd499b503b9fbcb165ab" "q-226150/selection/cee37ae0a2921bd3973a5cb0592c822dbbfafcfefba8d7007910ead797b9112f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-226150" :question/id #uuid "51d2761b-06a2-53c6-8491-c0e447a59ac2" :question/math-academy-id "q-226150" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/assets/80cc5e165f78bc1fc8cc7f83644fd00ca4a8f4e05e69d97b654bb2f1450d9b99.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=-x\\,i+3y\\,j$ and a rectangle $C$ with base $6$ and height $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/assets/511bae61d710c3362bb18618717b3d44ef5dcedece5f669e0b5237b7b524a054.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(-x\\,i+3y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=3i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}3\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=-12.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(-x\\,i+3y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+2\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =3{\\int }_{-3}^{3}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=36.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot (-12)+2\\cdot 36=48.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-226150/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "446c5e28-00d3-5a17-ad90-0e66b5ec3a59"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-226150"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
